WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:05.980
With Mission: Impossible Rogue Nation out
now, let’s take a look at 7 things you probably

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:10.789
didn’t know about the fifth film in the
super-spy series.

00:00:10.789 --> 00:00:15.610
Although Tom Cruise has a good deal of experience
stunt driving, before filming Rogue Nation's

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:20.912
high-speed car and motorbike chases along
winding Moroccan roads, he spent an intensive

00:00:20.912 --> 00:00:27.920
six weeks training, working with stunt co-ordinator
Wade Eastwood, whose speciality is car stunts.

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:35.090
Cruise learnt to drift drive and, according
to Eastwood, the actor rode the BMW S1000RR

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:40.250
sport bike at speeds of up to 100 miles per
hour!

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:46.170
When Ethan Hunt dives into an underwater facility
to change a security profile card, the chamber

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:49.570
where he has to swap out the card is labelled
108.

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:55.210
This is an Easter Egg for JJ Abrams' TV series
Lost, where the number 108, or a sequence

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:58.530
of numbers which added up to 108, often appeared.

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:04.860
Rogue Nation is produced by Bad Robot, a production
company founded by Abrams, who also directed

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:07.960
Mission: Impossible 3.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:13.110
The name of Swedish actress Rebecca Ferguson's
character Ilsa was inspired by a character

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:17.700
of the same name played by another Swedish
actress in a 1940s movie.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:23.090
That actress was Ingrid Bergman and the movie
was Casablanca, which starred Humphrey Bogart.

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:28.450
By the way, not so coincidentally, part of
Rogue Nation was actually filmed in Casablanca,

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:29.450
Morocco.

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:33.920
During Ferguson's first meeting with director
Chris McQuarrie and Tom Cruise, they compared

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:38.659
Ilsa in Mission: Impossible to Ingrid Bergman
and fellow Swedish actress Greta Garbo in

00:01:38.659 --> 00:01:43.930
terms of their grace and the use of light
and shadows in the way they were filmed.

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:50.030
To get ready for some especially dizzying
stunts, Rebecca Ferguson spent over a month

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:56.290
training in pilates, explosive running, fight
technique and firearms for 6 hours a day 6

00:01:56.290 --> 00:01:57.290
days a week.

00:01:57.290 --> 00:02:02.920
Her training also included numerous 12-foot
practice drops in the stunt rehearsal area

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:08.700
before she tackled the real 75-foot drop from
the roof of the Vienna State Opera House on

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:10.440
her first day of filming!

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:13.690
By the end of the shoot, Ferguson had done
that drop 40 times!

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:19.160
Oh, and by the way, she also had to overcome
her fear of heights and claustrophobia to

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:21.940
bring those stunts to life.

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:27.390
Remember the movie's opening stunt which Tom
Cruise actually did himself, hanging on to

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:33.270
the outside of an Airbus A-400-M military
transport plane as it flew up to 5,000 feet

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:34.270
in the air?

00:02:34.270 --> 00:02:38.720
Well, that scene was shot at the RAF Wittering
airbase in Cambridgeshire, England.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:43.620
A special rig was built to attach the camera
to the outside of the plane and the movie's

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:47.020
cinematographer operated it remotely from
inside the plane.

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:51.620
To capture additional footage from a different
angle, they also filmed from a helicopter

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:53.120
flying beside the plane.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:58.940
As the airplane was travelling up to 180 miles
per hour, the force of the wind was so great

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:03.960
during filming that Cruise had to have special
contact lenses made to cover his eyeballs

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:09.460
so he could keep his eyes open and still protect
them from engine fumes and airborne debris.

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:15.250
Cruise gave director Christopher McQuarrie
strict instructions not to cut once they started

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:19.880
filming unless he gave a signal to do so by
touching the top of his head!

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:24.690
In the end, Cruise did the stunt a total of
8 times to make sure they got all the shots

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:26.180
they needed.

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:32.710
To make the movie's underwater heist scene
as real as possible, Tom Cruise trained intensively

00:03:32.710 --> 00:03:37.230
in free diving, which is basically diving
without breathing apparatus!

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:42.970
Cruise built up to doing a 40-metre free dive
and could actually hold his breath underwater

00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:48.390
for around 6 minutes after training in Florida
and the Cayman Islands with veteran athletes

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:51.100
including freediving expert Kirk Krack.

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:56.580
The scene was filmed in a tank measuring about
40 foot square at Leavesden Studios in the

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:57.580
UK.

00:03:57.580 --> 00:04:02.430
For added realism, water movers from the Special
Effects department were used in the tank to

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:06.050
make Cruise's hair move and so he could swim
against the current.

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:10.190
However, the force of the moving water was
so strong that they had to tether Cruise to

00:04:10.190 --> 00:04:11.890
avoid him getting pushed backwards.

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:15.901
Cruise has said that the dive scene was the
most physically challenging part of the movie

00:04:15.901 --> 00:04:21.420
for him, and it took him significantly longer
to recover from than any other scene.

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:26.650
Although shooting a Mission: Impossible action
scene can be pretty intense, Tom Cruise and

00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:30.850
Simon Pegg still found time for fun on set
while they were filming a car chase scene

00:04:30.850 --> 00:04:32.060
in Casablanca.

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:36.470
The two actors kept themselves amused during
long stretches in the car in the scorching

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:41.850
Moroccan heat by playing pranks, including
sneakily switching on each other's car seat

00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:45.500
heater and waiting to see how long it took
the other one to notice!

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:50.210
Now, let me know in the comments below, what
crazy stunts would you like to see Tom Cruise

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:52.400
do next in Mission: Impossible 6?

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:55.650
And which is your favourite Mission: Impossible
movie and why?

